1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,614
Aarav switches on the stair light
at the bottom of the stairs.

2
00:00:03,914 --> 00:00:06,323
At the top, he flips a different switch.

3
00:00:06,323 --> 00:00:07,813
The same light goes off.

4
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:09,788
Two switches, one light.

5
00:00:09,788 --> 00:00:11,574
How do they share the job?

6
00:00:11,574 --> 00:00:12,631
Let's find out.

7
00:00:13,131 --> 00:00:15,299
A switch is like a tap for current.

8
00:00:15,649 --> 00:00:17,767
Close the tap, and water stops.

9
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:20,073
Open the switch, and current stops.

10
00:00:20,423 --> 00:00:23,940
Inside, a metal contact
makes the path, or breaks it.

11
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:27,144
Switches are named by poles and throws.

12
00:00:27,494 --> 00:00:30,889
A pole is one separate
circuit the switch controls.

13
00:00:31,539 --> 00:00:34,441
A throw is one path each
pole can connect to.

14
00:00:35,091 --> 00:00:37,125
Single pole, single throw.

15
00:00:37,125 --> 00:00:38,944
One circuit, one path.

16
00:00:38,944 --> 00:00:40,278
Just on or off.

17
00:00:40,628 --> 00:00:42,390
Single pole, double throw.

18
00:00:42,390 --> 00:00:45,121
One circuit, but two paths to choose from.

19
00:00:45,471 --> 00:00:47,121
Double pole, single throw.

20
00:00:47,121 --> 00:00:49,540
Two circuits switched on and off together.

21
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:51,455
Double pole, double throw.

22
00:00:51,455 --> 00:00:53,451
Two circuits, each with two paths.

23
00:00:54,101 --> 00:00:56,137
Now the stair light, on the breadboard.

24
00:00:56,487 --> 00:00:59,472
Each switch is single pole, double throw.

25
00:00:59,822 --> 00:01:01,178
Two wires join them.

26
00:01:01,178 --> 00:01:04,089
We call them the upper
wire and the lower wire.

27
00:01:04,439 --> 00:01:05,614
Both switches up.

28
00:01:05,614 --> 00:01:07,028
The path is complete.

29
00:01:07,028 --> 00:01:08,156
The light glows.

30
00:01:08,506 --> 00:01:10,130
Flip the top switch down.

31
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:11,239
The path breaks.

32
00:01:11,239 --> 00:01:12,583
The light goes dark.

33
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:15,056
Flip the bottom switch down too.

34
00:01:15,056 --> 00:01:16,881
The path is complete again.

35
00:01:17,231 --> 00:01:19,419
Now flip the top one back up.

36
00:01:19,419 --> 00:01:20,368
Dark again.

37
00:01:20,718 --> 00:01:24,066
So the light glows when both
switches point the same way.

38
00:01:24,716 --> 00:01:25,913
Watch the scope.

39
00:01:25,913 --> 00:01:29,162
The lamp voltage jumps
between zero and five volts.

40
00:01:29,812 --> 00:01:31,000
Next, the relay.

41
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,526
A relay is a switch worked by electricity.

42
00:01:33,876 --> 00:01:37,187
Think of a small child pressing
a bell for a strong guard.

43
00:01:37,537 --> 00:01:39,718
The guard then opens the heavy gate.

44
00:01:39,718 --> 00:01:41,298
Tiny effort, big result.

45
00:01:41,648 --> 00:01:44,005
Inside the relay is a coil of wire.

46
00:01:44,005 --> 00:01:45,573
It is called the coil.

47
00:01:45,923 --> 00:01:48,276
Current in the coil makes it a magnet.

48
00:01:48,276 --> 00:01:49,832
That is an electromagnet.

49
00:01:50,482 --> 00:01:53,852
The magnet pulls a metal
arm, called the armature.

50
00:01:54,202 --> 00:01:55,916
The arm moves a contact.

51
00:01:55,916 --> 00:01:58,130
Its moving part is called common.

52
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:03,002
With no coil current, common
touches the normally closed contact.

53
00:02:03,352 --> 00:02:04,726
Turn the coil on.

54
00:02:04,726 --> 00:02:07,567
Common jumps to the normally open contact.

55
00:02:08,217 --> 00:02:11,062
On the scope, the coil
voltage steps up first.

56
00:02:11,412 --> 00:02:14,574
A moment later, the load
voltage steps up too.

57
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:15,694
The pump runs.

58
00:02:16,344 --> 00:02:19,415
The coil circuit and the
pump circuit never touch.

59
00:02:19,415 --> 00:02:21,572
This keeps the small circuit safe.

60
00:02:22,222 --> 00:02:23,377
Last, connectors.

61
00:02:23,377 --> 00:02:25,801
They join wires and boards without solder.

62
00:02:26,151 --> 00:02:28,909
So a part can be unplugged
and replaced in seconds.

63
00:02:29,259 --> 00:02:32,305
Pin headers, screw terminals, and
plugs with sockets are common types.

64
00:02:32,655 --> 00:02:36,313
Many pin headers keep their pins two
point five four millimetres apart.

65
00:02:36,963 --> 00:02:40,513
Every switch, relay and connector
has ratings printed on it.

66
00:02:40,863 --> 00:02:44,998
The most important are the maximum
current and the maximum voltage.

67
00:02:45,648 --> 00:02:49,232
A small current in the coil
controls a big current in the load.

68
00:02:50,032 --> 00:02:51,078
Worked example.

69
00:02:51,078 --> 00:02:54,774
A relay coil is rated twelve
volts, with four hundred ohms.

70
00:02:55,124 --> 00:02:57,395
Coil current, I equals V by R.

71
00:02:57,395 --> 00:02:59,036
Twelve by four hundred.

72
00:02:59,736 --> 00:03:02,839
That is zero point zero three
amperes, or thirty milliamperes.

73
00:03:03,539 --> 00:03:05,461
Coil power is V times I.

74
00:03:05,461 --> 00:03:09,944
Twelve times zero point zero three
is zero point three six watts.

75
00:03:10,644 --> 00:03:12,690
The contacts are rated ten amperes.

76
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:16,393
Can they switch a fifteen hundred
watt heater on two thirty volts?

77
00:03:17,093 --> 00:03:18,833
Heater current is P by V.

78
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:22,585
Fifteen hundred by two thirty
is about six point five amperes.

79
00:03:23,285 --> 00:03:25,489
Six point five is less than ten.

80
00:03:25,489 --> 00:03:28,188
So yes, the relay can switch it safely.

81
00:03:28,838 --> 00:03:30,299
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:30,649 --> 00:03:34,394
Many students think normally open
means open while the relay is on.

83
00:03:34,744 --> 00:03:35,644
Wrong.

84
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:37,803
Normal means the coil has no current.

85
00:03:37,803 --> 00:03:40,364
Then the normally open contact is open.

86
00:03:41,014 --> 00:03:45,295
When the coil is energised, the
normally open contact closes.

87
00:03:45,945 --> 00:03:47,566
Where is this asked?

88
00:03:47,566 --> 00:03:49,426
In PUC board papers.

89
00:03:49,776 --> 00:03:54,599
Expect symbols of switch types, and
how a relay works, with a labelled

90
00:03:54,599 --> 00:03:55,499
diagram.

91
00:03:56,004 --> 00:03:56,904
Try this.

92
00:03:56,827 --> 00:04:01,430
A six volt relay coil has a resistance
of one hundred and twenty ohms.

93
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:05,251
It runs from a nine volt supply
through a series resistor.

94
00:04:05,251 --> 00:04:06,945
What resistor is needed?

95
00:04:12,345 --> 00:04:13,245
A.

96
00:04:12,715 --> 00:04:13,615
Sixty ohms.

97
00:04:13,894 --> 00:04:15,999
Coil current is six by one twenty.

98
00:04:15,999 --> 00:04:18,236
That is zero point zero five amperes.

99
00:04:18,586 --> 00:04:21,372
The resistor must drop
the extra three volts.

100
00:04:21,722 --> 00:04:24,763
Three by zero point
zero five is sixty ohms.

101
00:04:25,413 --> 00:04:26,959
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:30,159 --> 00:04:32,030
Next, cells and batteries.

103
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:35,693
Why does a phone battery say
volts and milliampere hours?
